# Files in this repo that are NOT part of the published site.
#
# This is what the Netlify `_redirects` rules did, done the way Cloudflare can:
# Netlify could only hide a repo file with a forced 404 rule (`404!`), because a
# deploy from the repository root uploads everything; an upload here is filtered
# first, and a path that was never deployed cannot be served by mistake. The
# rules file itself is gone with the rest of that configuration.
#
# `tools/audit.mjs` (check 16) asserts the pair stays complete: every tracked
# file is either intentionally public or listed here, and every pattern here
# matches something real - an ignore rule that matches nothing is a rule that
# would have silently stopped being true the day the file it was meant for
# returned. That second half exempts two kinds of pattern that a fresh clone
# cannot be expected to contain: local tool state (`.wrangler/`) and this
# repository's own deploy staging output (`.pages-dist/`).

# Repository and tooling. The assets layer does not exclude dot-entries by
# itself, so every one at the publish root is listed here.
.git/
.github/
.assetsignore
.gitignore

# Local tool state and OS noise - may not exist on a fresh clone; listed so
# that when they do exist they are never uploaded.
.wrangler/
.netlify/
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

tools/

README.md
_headers
wrangler.toml
# The function half of the site. Pages compiles this directory at deploy time -
# a file here is a route, not an asset - and the upload is a staged copy of the
# repository, so without this rule the source of every function would also be
# published as a static file under /functions/.
functions/
# Deploy files: the script that stages the upload and the staging output it
# writes (`.pages-dist/`) are development files. The assets directory is the
# repository root, so anything not listed here would be uploaded and served.
.pages-dist/
